ning up supplier names.\n\n\nCleaning the vendor name removes potentially problematic characters. The code filters garbage line items by checking description length and symbol ratios to exclude non-descriptive entries, removing common footer terms to avoid irrelevant data. Additionally, the line item quantity, rate, and total amount undergo data type validation and comma removal.\n\n\nApplying the sanitization function to the Gemini output is essential before updating the user interface state. The server-side prompt requires a detailed update to specifically target Indian GST Tax Invoices. This includes strict comma and number handling rules to prevent digit dropping, ensuring accurate extraction from both numerical figures and the \"Amount in Words\" field, while focusing on extracting only valid products/services, and accurately extracting the grand total from the invoice.\n\n\nEnsuring the total extracted from the invoice exactly matches the stated total, avoiding any calculation-based total. Quantity values will be validated as integers, while rates and billed amounts are checked to maintain the expected (quantity * rate) relationship. The `sanitize_and_clean_invoice` function is being defined to include supplier name cleansing by removing trailing symbols.\n\n\nRemoving leading/trailing brackets, vertical pipes, and whitespace is required to clean up extracted descriptions from the OCR process. Filtering out descriptions shorter than four letters or where symbols/punctuation exceed 20% is essential for data quality. The code now skips common footer/stamp terms to reduce noise.\n\nData type validation will be performed on quantity, rate, and billed amounts, with comma removal to prevent processing errors. If a value is missing, the default is set to 1.0 for quantity and 0.0 for rate. The billed amount is computed if not present. Fallback for column swaps in post-processing is considered if the total amount does not match.\n\n\nA potential column misalignment bet